implementationPlan.note:
The real-contention regression X14 (drafted in step-1's RED pass, same file) was checked against every step-3 requirement.
- Helper: one parameterized lockOwner(projectPath). A real child runs `node --import <absolute tsx loader URL> --input-type=module --eval`, importing source index.ts by absolute file URL (no dist), with explicit cwd = testDir's parent (testDir's drive on Windows), LOG_LEVEL=silent, and `acquireLock(spelling)`. It prints 'locked'/'refused' and stays alive on setInterval.
- No platform spoofing and no cwd spies. The parent does a real chdir to the same dir and asserts the alias precondition.
- Cases (it.each): owner canonical / contender alias, and owner alias / contender alias.
- Assertions:
  - the recorded lock pid equals the child pid, and isSameProjectPath(recorded, testDir) holds (canonical persisted);
  - the contender's acquireLock returns false;
  - the lock bytes are identical afterwards;
  - process.kill(owner, 0) is true and exitCode is null (owner alive).
  The contender never calls releaseLock.
- Bounds and cleanup:
  - readiness timer 30s; error and early-exit both reject (probe: a broken child import failed in 58ms with 'lock owner exited early (1)');
  - afterEach kill-and-await on every child via SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 5s; the per-test timeout is 60s;
  - outer afterEach closes the servers and removes testDir.
- Linux evidence (snapshot /tmp/moe-25ba-w093.ctB3E2):
  - RED against HEAD index.ts: both X14 cases 'expected true to be false';
  - GREEN with the step-2 fix: 5/5 consecutive runs of the alias block passed, about 0.65s per child;
  - no orphan processes (ps); 0 leftover fixture dirs.
- Retained controls passing: fresh/stale lock timeout, X3-X8 foreign, PID-mismatch stop.
- The native Windows run is in step-4.
